Mohammed Ali says that for decades his  family has had a shop on Baghdad's our  Rashid Street now the store and all his  stock of sportswear have been destroyed  during anti-government protests these  were not demonstrators there were  saboteurs the ones who throw Molotov  bombs and attack our shops and heritage  are not demonstrators the ones who  destroy property throw stones use  catapults on not demonstrators there's  been a street here since the Ottoman  Empire paved it in 1910 it became a hub  of Baghdad's Catholic society  particularly during the Iraq oil boom of  the 1950s when it was lined with West  Rong's clubs and cinemas but because of  its strategic importance it was blocked  during the 2003 Iraq war and stayed  closed for 15 years it finally reopened  in January 2019 only to become a  battleground months later as protestors  fought with security forces concrete  barriers blocked roads the nearby Sanok  and knocked our bridges across the  Tigris were closed around 400 shops were  burnt this is the core of Iraq's economy  shop owners here have lost millions of  dollars and they've stopped us doing our  jobs for the last five months buildings  which have been renovated as part of a  regeneration project now like gutted and  empty the demonstrations have really had  an impact on the projects they've  stopped all repair work including  geological surveys and the assessment of  the traditional houses there we've  counted 32 houses either burned or  damaged the bridges are open again and  the barriers are being removed but  business is still slow al Jazeera was  here on the day in January 2019 when all  of our sheet Street reopened after 15  years businesses were telling us that  they were optimistic the trade was going  to be in  the government at the time was hoping  that this was going to send a message  that Iraq was safe again but after  months of protests business owners in  our Rashid Street say that they're weary  of hoping that just because cynic bridge  is reopened and these concrete bollards  are starting to come down the business  is going to get any better  Hussein Ali's sunglasses shop lies in  the shadow of those barriers we're to  empty the store and close it and came in  to check on the shops and one had been  drenched by fire hoses it had been  burned the whole area has been damaged  people still worried about coming over  the sunnat' bridge once again times are  hard for the shopkeepers evolved Rashid  straight Rob Mathison
 

